Reviewer dnsd

We sincerely thank the reviewer for the thoughtful and technically careful comments. We have addressed each point carefully in the following.

W1 & Q3 — FNO vs our block

We appreciate the reviewer raising this point and clarify why our method is an instantiation of the FNO framework.

The original FNO layer [1] consists of three components: a learnable spectral convolution (forward FFT → multiply by learnable complex weights → inverse FFT), a local linear bypass, and a pointwise nonlinearity. Our frequency fusion module implements exactly these three components. The key distinction between FNO and plain FFT-based processing is the presence of learnable spectral weights that parameterize a global kernel in the frequency domain. Our module uses a learnable weight matrix to mix real and imaginary parts of the spectrum, which is the FNO's learnable spectral convolution, not a fixed or heuristic filter.
The original FNO applies spectral convolution along spatial axes because it learns spatial PDE operators. In our problem, the target mapping is from temporal input functions to temporal output functions, so applying the Fourier convolution along the time axis is the principled choice.
We will revise the manuscript to make this structural correspondence more explicit.

