W4 & Q4 — FNO and decoder design

We appreciate this concern. Our motivation for using the Fourier block is that SCADA and CERRA carry heterogeneous spatio-temporal patterns: SCADA reflects turbine-level fast local fluctuations, whereas CERRA captures smoother and larger-scale meteorological trends. We therefore hypothesize that learning their interaction in the frequency domain is more effective than relying only on direct time-domain mixing, because frequency-domain mixing can separate and recombine components at different temporal scales after modality alignment. In contrast, purely time-domain fusion may entangle these mismatched fluctuation trends and lead to negative transfer. In this sense, the NFL block is used here as an efficient mixer for heterogeneous multi-scale signals rather than as a claim that wind power forecasting is a strict PDE-operator learning task.

For the decoder part, we additionally compare the submitted MLP readout with stronger sequence decoders while keeping the rest of the pipeline unchanged.

